options:
  parameters:
    author: Matthieu
    catch_exceptions: 'True'
    category: '[GRC Hier Blocks]'
    cmake_opt: ''
    comment: "Dynamic range, and how to lose it - Fishball7020\n\nEvery control here is wired\
      \ to a number, so you can break the measurement on\npurpose and watch the cost. The\
      \ readout is peak minus noise floor on the trace\nyou are looking at.\n\nTHE ONE TO\
      \ TRY FIRST. Find any strong carrier. Set the window to Rectangular,\nthen to Blackman-Harris,\
      \ and watch the dynamic-range figure. On a synthetic\ntwo-tone test the difference measured\
      \ here was 78 dB: 40 bins away from a\nfull-scale carrier, a rectangular window leaves\
      \ a skirt at -42 dBFS and\nBlackman-Harris leaves -120 dBFS. Anything weaker than that\
      \ skirt does not\nexist as far as your spectrum is concerned. The cost is about two\
      \ bins of\nextra width - that is the whole trade.\n\nWHY THE FFT IS NOT THE STOCK QT\
      \ SINK. GNU Radio's frequency sink takes its\nwindow when it is built and offers no\
      \ way to change it while running, so you\ncould never watch the thing this example is\
      \ about. Doing the transform in an\nembedded block also means the dynamic-range number\
      \ comes from the very trace\non screen, so the picture and the number cannot disagree.\n\
      \n  LO offset    drags the receiver's own LO leak out of the middle of the\n       \
      \        span. Set it to 0 and the spike lands on whatever you were\n              \
      \ trying to measure. It is a FRACTION of the span, so it stays\n               sensible\
      \ when you change the sample rate.\n  Gain mode    manual, or let the AGC decide. Switch\
      \ to slow_attack and the\n               vertical axis stops meaning anything: the receiver\
      \ is now\n               changing its own reference level while you read it.\n  Averaging\
      \    a one-pole average over FFT frames, in the POWER domain.\n  Floor %      which\
      \ percentile counts as 'noise'. Raise it through a busy\n               band and watch\
      \ the floor climb into the signals, because a\n               percentile cannot tell\
      \ noise from traffic.\n\nLEVELS ARE dBFS, not dBm - decibels relative to the converter's\
      \ full scale.\nNothing in this repository is calibrated to absolute power."
    copyright: ''
    description: Spectrum, waterfall and a live dynamic-range readout
    gen_cmake: 'On'
    gen_linking: dynamic
    generate_options: qt_gui
    hier_block_src_path: '.:'
    id: fishball_dynamic_range
    max_nouts: '0'
    output_language: python
    placement: (0,0)
    qt_qss_theme: ''
    realtime_scheduling: ''
    run: 'True'
    run_command: '{python} -u {filename}'
    run_options: prompt
    sizing_mode: fixed
    thread_safe_setters: ''
    title: Fishball7020 - dynamic range, and how to lose it
    window_size: (1800,1100)
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 8
    - 8
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
blocks:
- name: import_math
  id: import
  parameters:
