- name: note_rate
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: 'Sample rate and buffer size are a trade, not a setting.


      Dynamic range is BETTER at a lower rate: the same 4096-point FFT over a

      narrower span puts less noise in each bin. 5 MS/s is the default for that

      reason, not to be gentle on the link.


      Push the chooser to 61.44 MS/s and two things happen. The span gets 12x

      wider and every bin gets 11 dB noisier - and the stream needs 245 MB/s,

      which no Ethernet link carries. Samples will be dropped. Raise `buf` to

      1048576 or more first: a bigger libiio buffer was measured to be worth about

      3x the throughput over a network. See docs/modulation-and-throughput.md.


      `buf` is fixed when the flowgraph starts, so changing it needs a re-run.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 340
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_gain
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: "The gain slider goes to 71, and above 4 GHz that is a lie.\n\nThe AD9361's gain\
      \ table depends on the band: full scale is 71 dB at 2.4 GHz\nbut 62 dB above 4 GHz.\
      \ gr-iio only LOGS a refusal from the driver, so a value\npast the end of the table\
      \ leaves the gain wherever it was and nothing on\nscreen says so. If a level looks stuck,\
      \ check the gain actually took:\n\n    # run from: the repo root\n    ./devkit status\n\
      \nRX2 is off in this example. Turn it on and you double the data rate for a\nchannel\
      \ you are not looking at."
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 420
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: rx
  id: iio_fmcomms2_source
  parameters:
    type: fc32
    uri: uri
    frequency: int(center_hz - lo_off_hz)
    samplerate: samp_rate
    buffer_size: buf
    rx1_en: 'True'
    rx2_en: 'False'
    quadrature: 'True'
    rfdc: 'True'
    bbdc: 'True'
    gain1: gain_mode
    manual_gain1: rx_gain
    gain2: '''manual'''
    manual_gain2: '20'
    rf_port_select: '''A_BALANCED'''
    filter_source: '''Auto'''
    filter: ''
    fpass: '0'
    fstop: '0'
    bandwidth: int(samp_rate)
    len_tag_key: packet_len
    comment: 'RX1 only. The LO sits lo_off_hz BELOW the frequency you

      asked for; the rotator downstream shifts it back, which

      leaves the LO leak off to one side instead of on top of

      your signal. Quadrature and both DC loops on: they are

      what keeps the image and the leak from growing.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 400
    - 400
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: shift_back
  id: blocks_rotator_cc
  parameters:
    phase_inc: -2 * math.pi * lo_frac
    tag_inc_update: 'False'
    comment: 'Undo the LO offset digitally, so the x-axis reads true

      frequency. phase_inc is just -2*pi*lo_frac: the offset in

      cycles per sample IS the fraction of the span.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 672
    - 424
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: to_frames
  id: blocks_stream_to_vector
  parameters:
    type: complex
    num_items: nfft
    vlen: '1'
    comment: One FFT frame per item from here on.
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 872
    - 424
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: drop_frames
  id: blocks_keep_one_in_n
  parameters:
    type: complex
    n: max(1, int(samp_rate / (nfft * frames)))
    vlen: nfft
    comment: 'Keep a dozen frames a second and DROP the rest.

      Deliberate: a Python FFT cannot keep up with 1200

      frames/s, and a block that cannot keep up applies

      backpressure all the way to the radio, which turns into

      dropped buffers you did not ask for. Dropping on purpose

      is cheaper and visible.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 1048
    - 424
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: dr
  id: epy_block
  parameters:
    nfft: nfft
    window: fft_win
    avg: avg
    hold: hold
    floor_pct: floor_pct
    comment: 'Window, FFT, power averaging, max hold and the

      dynamic-range metric. examples/lib/spectrum_engine.py -

      edit it there and re-run examples/mkgrc.py.'
    _source_code: "\"\"\"Spectrum, max-hold, and the dynamic range you are actually getting.\n\
      \nThis block exists because GNU Radio's stock QT frequency sink takes its FFT\nwindow\
      \ as a constructor argument and offers no callback to change it. You\ncannot switch\
      \ window while it runs, so you cannot *watch* what the window\ncosts you - which is\
      \ the whole lesson of example 01. Doing the transform here\nbuys three things at once:\n\
      \n  * the window changes while the flowgraph runs,\n  * the averaging happens in the\
      \ POWER domain, where it belongs,\n  * the dynamic-range number is computed from the\
      \ very trace on screen,\n    so the picture and the number can never disagree.\n\nThat\
      \ last point is not hypothetical. An earlier tool in this repository scored\na max-hold\
      \ trace against a noise floor derived from an *averaged* trace, and\nreported about\
      \ 13 dB more dynamic range than the board had. Two traces, two\ndifferent noise floors,\
      \ one number: it was wrong and it looked plausible.\nThe metric here is taken from one\
      \ trace only, and `hold` is display-only.\n\nFULL SCALE. gr-iio's fc32 sources hand\
      \ you samples already normalised, so a\ntone at the converter's full scale arrives as\
      \ amplitude 1.0 - it is NOT raw\nconverter LSBs. Everything below is therefore dBFS:\
      \ decibels relative to full\nscale, not dBm. There is no calibration to absolute power\
      \ anywhere in this\nrepository, and a dBFS figure says nothing about what is at the\
      \ antenna until\nyou add one.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom gnuradio import gr\n\
      \n#: Amplitude of a full-scale tone as gr-iio's fc32 source presents it.\n#:\n#: Measured\
      \ rather than assumed, because a wrong guess here shifts every level\n#: in this repository's\
      \ examples by 24 dB and nothing would look broken. The\n#: same signal was captured\
      \ twice back to back, once with `iio_readdev` (raw\n#: converter counts) and once through\
      \ gr-iio's fc32 source: per-component RMS\n#: 5.1006 counts against 0.002509, a ratio\
      \ of 2033. That is 2048 to within the\n#: drift between two captures of thermal noise\
      \ - so gr-iio divides out the\n#: 12-bit converter's +/-2048 full scale (see RX_FULL_SCALE\
      \ in\n#: tools/selftest/sdr_selftest.py) and hands you +/-1.0.\nFULL_SCALE = 1.0\n\n\
      #: Sidelobe level of each window, for the label in the GUI. The whole point of\n#: the\
      \ control: a rectangular window's first sidelobe is 13 dB down, so a\n#: strong carrier\
      \ smears over everything within a few bins of it and a weak\n#: neighbour simply is\
      \ not there. Blackman-Harris pays about 2 bins of extra\n#: width to put that skirt\
      \ 92 dB down.\nSIDELOBES_DB = {'rectangular': -13, 'hann': -31, 'blackman-harris': -92}\n\
      \n\ndef make_window(name, n):\n    \"\"\"One of three windows, by name. numpy only -\
      \ no scipy dependency.\"\"\"\n    if name == 'rectangular':\n        return np.ones(n)\n\
      \    k = np.arange(n)\n    if name == 'hann':\n        return 0.5 - 0.5 * np.cos(2 *\
      \ np.pi * k / n)\n    if name == 'blackman-harris':\n        a = (0.35875, 0.48829,\
      \ 0.14128, 0.01168)   # 4-term, -92 dB sidelobes\n        return (a[0] - a[1] * np.cos(2\
      \ * np.pi * k / n)\n                + a[2] * np.cos(4 * np.pi * k / n)\n           \
      \     - a[3] * np.cos(6 * np.pi * k / n))\n    raise ValueError('unknown window %r -\
      \ one of %s'\n                     % (name, sorted(SIDELOBES_DB)))\n\n\nclass blk(gr.sync_block):\n\
      \    \"\"\"Spectrum + dynamic range\n\n    Takes one complex vector per FFT and produces:\n\
      \n      0  averaged spectrum, dBFS      1  max-hold spectrum, dBFS\n      2  dynamic\
      \ range, dB            3  peak, dBFS\n      4  noise floor, dBFS            5  window\
      \ sidelobe level, dB\n\n    DYNAMIC RANGE here means peak minus noise floor on the averaged\
      \ trace,\n    and 'noise floor' means the `floor_pct` percentile of that same trace.\n\
      \    That definition is a choice, and the slider exists so you can see it is a\n   \
      \ choice: raise the percentile through a band that is half occupied and the\n    'floor'\
      \ climbs into the signals, because a percentile cannot tell noise\n    from traffic.\
      \ It is honest for a quiet band and optimistic for a busy one.\n\n    AVERAGING is a\
      \ one-pole IIR on power, p_avg += (p - p_avg) / avg. Averaging\n    power is correct\
      \ and averaging dB is not: dB is logarithmic, so a mean of\n    dB values is a geometric\
      \ mean of powers and biases every noisy bin low.\n\n    FFT SIZE is fixed when the flowgraph\
      \ starts. It sets the width of this\n    block's vector ports, and GNU Radio fixes port\
      \ widths at construction, so\n    no amount of GUI would make it live. Edit the `nfft`\
      \ variable and re-run.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, nfft=4096, window='blackman-harris',\
      \ avg=16,\n                 hold=True, floor_pct=10.0):\n        gr.sync_block.__init__(\n\
      \            self, name='Spectrum + dynamic range',\n            in_sig=[(np.complex64,\
      \ nfft)],\n            out_sig=[(np.float32, nfft), (np.float32, nfft),\n          \
      \           np.float32, np.float32, np.float32, np.float32])\n        # Deliberately\
      \ _nfft, not nfft: GRC turns every __init__ argument that\n        # is also an instance\
      \ attribute into a live setter, and a setter for\n        # nfft would be a lie - it\
      \ cannot resize the ports.\n        self._nfft = int(nfft)\n        self._avg = max(1.0,\
      \ float(avg))\n        self._hold = bool(hold)\n        self._floor_pct = float(np.clip(floor_pct,\
      \ 0.1, 99.9))\n        self._acc = None\n        self._peak = None\n        self._win_name\
      \ = None\n        self.window = window          # property: builds coefficients and\
      \ norm\n\n    # ---- live controls --------------------------------------------------\n\
      \    # Properties rather than plain attributes so the window is rebuilt once\n    #\
      \ per change instead of once per FFT, and so the clamping cannot be\n    # bypassed\
      \ by the GUI handing us a value from the edge of a slider.\n\n    @property\n    def\
      \ window(self):\n        return self._win_name\n\n    @window.setter\n    def window(self,\
      \ name):\n        w = make_window(name, self._nfft)\n        self._win_name = name\n\
      \        self._win = w.astype(np.float32)\n        # A window has gain of its own and\
      \ it has to be divided back out or\n        # every level is wrong. Dividing by (sum\
      \ w)^2 puts a full-scale tone\n        # at 0 dBFS for every window, so the traces stay\
      \ comparable when you\n        # switch - which is the only way the control teaches\
      \ anything.\n        self._norm = float(w.sum()) ** 2 * FULL_SCALE ** 2\n        self._sidelobe\
      \ = float(SIDELOBES_DB.get(name, 0))\n\n    @property\n    def avg(self):\n        return\
      \ self._avg\n\n    @avg.setter\n    def avg(self, v):\n        self._avg = max(1.0,\
      \ float(v))\n\n    @property\n    def hold(self):\n        return self._hold\n\n   \
      \ @hold.setter\n    def hold(self, v):\n        self._hold = bool(v)\n        if not\
      \ self._hold:\n            self._peak = None         # unticking the box is also the\
      \ reset\n\n    @property\n    def floor_pct(self):\n        return self._floor_pct\n\
      \n    @floor_pct.setter\n    def floor_pct(self, v):\n        self._floor_pct = float(np.clip(v,\
      \ 0.1, 99.9))\n\n    # ---- work -----------------------------------------------------------\n\
      \    def work(self, input_items, output_items):\n        x = input_items[0]\n      \
      \  n = len(x)\n        for i in range(n):\n            spec = np.fft.fftshift(np.fft.fft(x[i]\
      \ * self._win))\n            p = (spec.real ** 2 + spec.imag ** 2) / self._norm\n\n\
      \            if self._acc is None or self._acc.shape != p.shape:\n                self._acc\
      \ = p.copy()\n            else:\n                self._acc += (p - self._acc) / self._avg\n\
      \n            if self._hold:\n                self._peak = p.copy() if self._peak is\
      \ None \\\n                    else np.maximum(self._peak, p)\n            shown = self._peak\
      \ if self._peak is not None else self._acc\n\n            # 1e-30 keeps an empty bin\
      \ from becoming -inf and taking the whole\n            # display's autoscale with it.\n\
      \            avg_db = 10.0 * np.log10(self._acc + 1e-30)\n            output_items[0][i]\
      \ = avg_db\n            output_items[1][i] = 10.0 * np.log10(shown + 1e-30)\n\n    \
      \        # Peak and floor from the SAME trace. See the class docstring.\n          \
      \  floor = 10.0 * np.log10(\n                np.percentile(self._acc, self._floor_pct)\
      \ + 1e-30)\n            peak = float(avg_db.max())\n            output_items[2][i] =\
      \ peak - floor\n            output_items[3][i] = peak\n            output_items[4][i]\
      \ = floor\n            output_items[5][i] = self._sidelobe\n        return n\n"
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 1240
    - 396
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
    _io_cache: '(''Spectrum + dynamic range'', ''blk'', [(''nfft'', ''4096''), (''window'',
      "''blackman-harris''"), (''avg'', ''16''), (''hold'', ''True''), (''floor_pct'', ''10.0'')],
      [(''0'', ''complex'', 4096)], [(''0'', ''float'', 4096), (''1'', ''float'', 4096), (''2'',
      ''float'', 1), (''3'', ''float'', 1), (''4'', ''float'', 1), (''5'', ''float'', 1)],
      "Spectrum + dynamic range\n\n    Takes one complex vector per FFT and produces:\n\n      0  averaged
      spectrum, dBFS      1  max-hold spectrum, dBFS\n      2  dynamic range, dB            3  peak,
      dBFS\n      4  noise floor, dBFS            5  window sidelobe level, dB\n\n    DYNAMIC
      RANGE here means peak minus noise floor on the averaged trace,\n    and ''noise floor''
      means the `floor_pct` percentile of that same trace.\n    That definition is a choice,
      and the slider exists so you can see it is a\n    choice: raise the percentile through
      a band that is half occupied and the\n    ''floor'' climbs into the signals, because
      a percentile cannot tell noise\n    from traffic. It is honest for a quiet band and
      optimistic for a busy one.\n\n    AVERAGING is a one-pole IIR on power, p_avg += (p
      - p_avg) / avg. Averaging\n    power is correct and averaging dB is not: dB is logarithmic,
      so a mean of\n    dB values is a geometric mean of powers and biases every noisy bin
      low.\n\n    FFT SIZE is fixed when the flowgraph starts. It sets the width of this\n    block''s
      vector ports, and GNU Radio fixes port widths at construction, so\n    no amount of
      GUI would make it live. Edit the `nfft` variable and re-run.\n    ", [''avg'', ''floor_pct'',
      ''hold'', ''window''])'
- name: spectrum
  id: qtgui_vector_sink_f
  parameters:
    name: '''Spectrum - average and max hold'''
    vlen: nfft
    x_start: center_mhz - samp_rate / 2e6
    x_step: samp_rate / nfft / 1e6
    x_axis_label: '''frequency'''
    y_axis_label: '''level'''
    x_units: '''MHz'''
    y_units: '''dBFS'''
    ref_level: '0'
    grid: 'True'
    autoscale: 'False'
    average: '1.0'
    ymin: '-140'
    ymax: '0'
    nconnections: '2'
    update_time: '0.10'
    showports: 'False'
    legend: 'True'
    label1: '''average'''
    width1: '1'
    color1: '''blue'''
    alpha1: '1.0'
    label2: '''max hold'''
    width2: '1'
    color2: '''red'''
    alpha2: '0.6'
    gui_hint: 0,2,6,10
    comment: 'Two traces from one engine: the average and the max hold.'
  states:
